  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nA simple summertime weather regime will continue through\nTuesday, as strong midlevel high pressure expands and\nstrengthens over SW KS. The only impact to consider is exactly\nhow hot the afternoons will get. NBM has been too cool now for \na few days, running about 4-5 degrees below reality. \n00z NAM MOS appears to be capturing thermal trends correctly,\nwith all models showing heights, thickness and 850 mb\ntemperatures all increasing through Tuesday. An upper high near\n597 dm is projected near DDC by Tuesday afternoon, with the\nafternoon heat continuing to build. Grids will carry\ntemperatures near 100 at 4 pm for all zones Monday, and as \nhigh as 103-105 Tuesday directly under the core of the ridge's\nsubsidence. This said, some guidance appears too hot Tuesday,\nwith a weak cool front/NEly wind shift sliding into SW KS during\nthe afternoon. It's hard to believe the hottest guidance given\npoor mixing and lack of downslope. Regardless, significant heat\nimpacts are expected both Monday and Tuesday afternoons, with\nheat advisories necessary where heat indices meet or exceed 105\n(generally east of US 283). Maintained the inherited heat \nadvisory for the eastern zones Monday. Another heat advisory is \nexpected to be required for at least Barber county and vicinity \nTuesday, pending the expected NEly wind shift progress. \n\nAfter several afternoons with a healthy cumulus field and even\nsome small rain showers, subsidence will increase strongly\nMonday and Tuesday, limiting cumulus and keeping pops at/near\nzero. \n\nModel guidance allows the center of the 597 dm ridge to sag\nsouthward Wednesday through Wednesday night, with the ridge axis\nsettling near the Red River valley of North Texas. This\nevolution will allow for the heat to relax several degrees, back\ninto the 90s, so a heat advisory does not look necessary.\nDaylight Wednesday will remain dry, but a weak shortwave wave\nrounding the northern periphery of the southward-displaced ridge\nwill likely ignite a thunderstorm complex/MCS across Nebraska\nand northern Kansas Wednesday night. Some of this may reach the\nnorthern zones, north of K-96, as NBM pops suggest. \n\nThursday through the upcoming weekend, the ridge is expected to\nretrograde westward back to New Mexico, with heights near 600 dm\nover New Mexico by Saturday night. Another shortwave rounding\nthe northeast periphery of the ridge may allow more\nthunderstorms into western Kansas Thursday night as global \nmodels and NBM pops suggest. Otherwise, the hot afternoons \ntypical of July will continue.",
